OPAL

Performance-first Linux + Apple Silicon macOS remote desktop.

Fresh install - Linux

1. Install build/runtime dependencies.

Fedora 44+ / Fedora Asahi
  sudo dnf install -y https://mirrors.rpmfusion.org/free/fedora/rpmfusion-free-release-$(rpm -E %fedora).noarch.rpm
  sudo dnf install -y --allowerasing gcc-c++ make git pkgconf-pkg-config SDL3-devel openssl-devel ffmpeg ffmpeg-devel pipewire pipewire-devel pipewire-pulseaudio libportal libportal-devel xdg-desktop-portal xdg-desktop-portal-kde wl-clipboard curl

CachyOS / Arch Linux
  sudo pacman -S --needed base-devel git pkgconf sdl3 openssl ffmpeg pipewire pipewire-pulse libportal xdg-desktop-portal xdg-desktop-portal-kde wl-clipboard curl

Debian 13+ / Ubuntu with SDL3
  sudo apt-get update
  sudo apt-get install -y build-essential git pkg-config libsdl3-dev libssl-dev ffmpeg libavformat-dev libavcodec-dev libavutil-dev libswresample-dev libswscale-dev libpipewire-0.3-dev libportal-dev pipewire pipewire-pulse xdg-desktop-portal xdg-desktop-portal-kde wl-clipboard curl

2. Install and join Tailscale.

  curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh
  sudo tailscale up
  tailscale ip -4
  tailscale status

The device must be authenticated into the same Tailnet as the other OPAL computer. tailscale ip -4 should print a 100.x.x.x address before testing OPAL. OPAL can still use LAN/rendezvous/relay without Tailscale, but direct Tailnet discovery requires a connected Tailscale client.

3. Clone OPAL.

  git clone https://github.com/xt9y/OPAL.git
  cd OPAL

4. Build and install.

  make -j"$(nproc)"
  sudo make install
  hash -r

5. Verify.

  opal doctor
  tailscale ip -4
  tailscale status

6. First run / Linux screen authorization.

  opal

Choose "Host this computer" on the machine being controlled.

On Wayland, OPAL asks:

  Remember selected screens for automatic hosting? [Y/n]

Answer Y to open the desktop screen-sharing chooser once. On KDE, select every monitor that OPAL should be allowed to share. The selection is stored through the XDG ScreenCast persistent restore token under ~/.opal and is reused by the long-lived host capture session.

If multiple monitors are selected, OPAL captures all selected portal streams and presents them to the client as equal-width/equal-height tiles in one horizontal row. Mixed monitor resolutions are scaled to the same tile size. The finished combined desktop is encoded as one H.264 stream, so clients do not need a separate multi-monitor protocol.

Normal bitrate changes, IDR/decode recovery, media sender restart, client reconnect and capture-stale recovery do not intentionally recreate the KDE ScreenCast portal session. They reuse the already-authorized persistent PipeWire capture session, so the monitor/window chooser should not reappear during an ordinary live session.

If KDE/xdg-desktop-portal revokes or invalidates the stored authorization, OPAL reports that Linux screen authorization was lost instead of deliberately opening a new chooser from the media recovery path. Run host setup again to authorize screens again.

Relative/raw mouse movement is unchanged. Absolute desktop pointer input is inverse-mapped through the combined monitor layout so clicks on each client tile target the corresponding physical KDE monitor correctly.

For diagnostics:

  OPAL_DEBUG=1 opal

Fresh install - macOS

Requirements: Apple Silicon, macOS 13+ and Homebrew.

1. Install Xcode Command Line Tools and dependencies.

  xcode-select --install
  brew install make pkg-config sdl3 openssl@3 ffmpeg git

Apple's /usr/bin/make is older than OPAL's build requires. You still use the normal make command: OPAL automatically forwards macOS builds to Homebrew gmake internally.

2. Install and join Tailscale.

Recommended for a new OPAL CLI/headless installation:

  brew install tailscale
  sudo brew services start tailscale
  sudo tailscale up
  tailscale ip -4
  tailscale status

sudo tailscale up opens/authenticates the machine into your Tailnet. Starting tailscaled alone is not enough. tailscale ip -4 should print a 100.x.x.x address and tailscale status should show the other OPAL machine before testing Tailnet connectivity.

If you already use the normal Tailscale.app and it is signed in, OPAL also detects its bundled CLI at /Applications/Tailscale.app/Contents/MacOS/Tailscale. A separate Homebrew Tailscale installation is not required in that case. OPAL prefers a Tailscale instance that is actually connected, so an installed-but-logged-out CLI should not mask a connected app.

3. Clone OPAL.

  git clone https://github.com/xt9y/OPAL.git
  cd OPAL

4. One-step build/sign/install.

  make install
  hash -r

Do not run sudo make install on macOS. make install builds and signs as your normal user and invokes sudo only for the final copies into /usr/local.

5. Verify the installation and Tailscale.

  opal doctor
  tailscale ip -4
  tailscale status

6. macOS host permissions.

Run:

  opal

Choose "Host this computer". OPAL requests permission for the exact processes that need it.

System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Screen & System Audio Recording
  allow OPAL / opal

System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Accessibility
  allow opal-input

If macOS asks for permission, grant it and run opal again to finish host setup.

The host service is installed as:

  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/de.xt9y.opal.host.plist

Restart it with:

  opal restart

Re-check everything with:

  opal doctor

7. Optional macOS verification.

Permission-free build/unit/sign verification:

  make test

Native ScreenCaptureKit -> VideoToolbox/AAC verification after Screen Recording is granted:

  make macos-runtime-test

Run / connect

  setup / select host --> opal
  connect             --> opal <connection-code>
  debug               --> OPAL_DEBUG=1 opal <connection-code>
  doctor              --> opal doctor
  restart host        --> opal restart

If OPAL says Tailscale is installed but disconnected:

  sudo tailscale up
  tailscale ip -4
  tailscale status

If OPAL cannot find a host on Tailscale, verify that both machines have 100.x addresses and appear in each other's tailscale status output before debugging OPAL itself.

Linux host
  capture   --> persistent PipeWire + libportal + xdg-desktop-portal, multi-monitor composition
  encode    --> persistent in-process FFmpeg/hardware H.264 path
  input     --> /dev/uinput with combined-desktop absolute pointer mapping
  clipboard --> wl-clipboard on Wayland, SDL fallback elsewhere
  audio     --> PipeWire/PulseAudio-compatible source
  UDP       --> sendmmsg/recvmmsg batching

macOS host
  capture   --> ScreenCaptureKit, latest-frame CVPixelBuffer path with WindowServer display timestamps
  encode    --> persistent hardware VideoToolbox H.264 low-latency rate control, zero frame delay, on-demand IDR
  input     --> CGEvent through Accessibility-authorized opal-input, spawned with posix_spawn
  clipboard --> NSPasteboard
  audio     --> ScreenCaptureKit system audio + persistent in-process AAC using the stream synchronization clock
  service   --> per-user launchd LaunchAgent
  UDP       --> nonblocking bounded datagram loop

client
  window/events/input/audio output --> SDL3
  H.264 decode                     --> libavcodec
  presentation                     --> SDL3
  macOS clipboard                 --> NSPasteboard

network
  signed rendezvous + direct end-to-end encrypted UDP + blind encrypted relay fallback
  Tailscale is used when available for the direct tailnet path; local address discovery uses tailscale ip -4
  Linux keeps native UDP batching; macOS uses the same batch contract without packet-copy emulation

verify
  Linux  --> make test && make test-hpi
  macOS build/unit/sign --> make test
  macOS native media   --> make macos-runtime-test
  macOS host readiness --> opal doctor

  host   --> OPAL_DEBUG=1 opal
  client --> OPAL_DEBUG=1 opal <connection-code>

Windows
  not implemented yet; platform/core interfaces are kept Windows-compatible for a later backend

server
  make deploy-rendezvous
